The factory is a single registry that maps a requested type to the type that is actually built. A class joins it through the `uvm_object_utils` or `uvm_component_utils` macro, which declares a `type_id` proxy for the class. A call to `T::type_id::create(...)` asks the factory for a T. The factory looks for an override and returns either T or a registered subclass of T. A call to `new()` always builds exactly T, so no override can ever reach it.

The payoff: a test changes what the environment builds without editing the environment. Here one test swaps every bus driver, or just one, for an error-injecting subclass:

```systemverilog
class err_test extends base_test;
  `uvm_component_utils(err_test)
  function new(string name, uvm_component parent);
    super.new(name, parent);
  endfunction
  function void build_phase(uvm_phase phase);
    // Overrides must exist before the targets are created.
    // Type override: every bus_driver becomes an err_driver ...
    bus_driver::type_id::set_type_override(err_driver::get_type());
    // ... or an instance override: only this path (relative to the test, because parent = this)
    // bus_driver::type_id::set_inst_override(err_driver::get_type(), "env.agt0.drv", this);
    super.build_phase(phase);   // builds env; env and agents build the drivers later, top-down
  endfunction
endclass
```

Rules that follow from how create() works:

1. Overrides apply at creation time. An object created before the override was registered keeps its type.

2. Instance overrides beat type overrides, and overrides chain: if B overrides A and C overrides B, a request for A builds C.

3. The override type must extend the original. If it does not, the typed `create()` cannot cast the result and uvm-core reports a fatal FCTTYP.

4. Only factory-created objects are affected. Debug with `uvm_factory::get().print()`, which lists registered types and active overrides.

Each task phase owns an objection: a counter (`uvm_objection`) that says "do not end this phase yet". `raise_objection` adds to it, `drop_objection` subtracts, and the counts propagate up the hierarchy to the top. The usual place is the test, around the top-level sequence:

```systemverilog
task run_phase(uvm_phase phase);
  top_seq seq = top_seq::type_id::create("seq");
  phase.raise_objection(this, "running top_seq");   // at time 0, before any delay
  seq.start(env.agt.sqr);
  phase.drop_objection(this, "top_seq finished");
endtask
```

When the total reaches zero, the phase waits for any drain time, gives components a chance to object again in `phase_ready_to_end`, and then ends. When a task phase ends, UVM kills the processes it started, which is why a driver's `forever` loop does not keep the test alive and should not raise objections.

Forgot to drop: the phase never ends by objection. Simulation time keeps advancing, so the run-phase watchdog eventually fires `uvm_fatal` with ID PH_TIMEOUT. The default limit is `UVM_DEFAULT_TIMEOUT`, 9200 s of simulated time; change it with `uvm_root::set_timeout()` or the uvm-core plusarg `+UVM_TIMEOUT`. It is not an infinite hang unless simulation time itself stops advancing.

Never raised: after one NBA region with no objection, the phase ends immediately. `+UVM_PHASE_TRACE` shows "No objections raised, skipping phase". The test finishes at time 0, often with `UVM_ERROR : 0`, having checked nothing. An objection raised after a delay is too late for the same reason.

Drain time (`set_drain_time`) is a delay that starts after the count reaches zero. It lets in-flight transactions reach the scoreboard. It cannot rescue a forgotten drop, because a forgotten drop never reaches zero.

The usual hierarchy:

```text
uvm_test_top            (test: picks config and sequences)
  env                   (uvm_env: agents plus checkers)
    agt                 (uvm_agent: one interface)
      sqr  -> drv  ---> DUT pins      (active only)
      mon  <--- DUT pins
            \--ap--> scb (uvm_scoreboard, in env)
             \-ap--> cov (uvm_subscriber, in env)
```

The agent bundles everything for one interface. The scoreboard sits in the env, not in the agent, because it usually compares traffic from more than one agent.

An active agent drives the interface: it builds a sequencer, a driver and a monitor. A passive agent only observes: it builds the monitor (plus any checks and coverage) and no driver or sequencer at all. You use passive mode when something else drives the interface, for example when the block's bus is driven by the real CPU at SoC level.

```systemverilog
class bus_agent extends uvm_agent;
  `uvm_component_utils(bus_agent)
  bus_sequencer sqr;
  bus_driver    drv;
  bus_monitor   mon;
  function new(string name, uvm_component parent);
    super.new(name, parent);
  endfunction
  function void build_phase(uvm_phase phase);
    super.build_phase(phase);   // uvm_agent reads the "is_active" setting here
    mon = bus_monitor::type_id::create("mon", this);
    if (get_is_active() == UVM_ACTIVE) begin
      sqr = bus_sequencer::type_id::create("sqr", this);
      drv = bus_driver::type_id::create("drv", this);
    end
  endfunction
  function void connect_phase(uvm_phase phase);
    if (get_is_active() == UVM_ACTIVE)
      drv.seq_item_port.connect(sqr.seq_item_export);
  endfunction
endclass

// In the env or test: make one agent passive
uvm_config_db#(uvm_active_passive_enum)::set(this, "env.agt", "is_active", UVM_PASSIVE);
```

`is_active` defaults to `UVM_ACTIVE`. In uvm-core 2020.3.1, `uvm_agent::build_phase` reads the `is_active` configuration, so a derived agent must call `super.build_phase(phase)` before it calls `get_is_active()`.

The roles: a sequence item is one transaction (an object). A sequence is an object whose `body()` creates items. The sequencer is a component that arbitrates between all sequences running on it and hands one item at a time to the driver. The driver is a component that pulls items and turns them into pin activity.

```systemverilog
class wr_seq extends uvm_sequence #(bus_item);
  `uvm_object_utils(wr_seq)
  function new(string name = "wr_seq");
    super.new(name);
  endfunction
  task body();
    repeat (4) begin
      bus_item req = bus_item::type_id::create("req");
      start_item(req);                              // wait for the sequencer's grant
      if (!req.randomize() with { write == 1; })    // late randomization, after the grant
        `uvm_error("RNDFAIL", "bus_item randomize failed")
      finish_item(req);                             // wait for the driver's item_done()
    end
  endtask
endclass
// test: seq.start(env.agt.sqr);
```

`start_item()` returns when the sequencer grants this sequence. Arbitration happens when the driver asks for an item, and the default policy, `UVM_SEQ_ARB_FIFO`, grants requests in arrival order. `start_item()` does not randomize anything.

`finish_item()` sends the item to the driver and returns when the driver signals completion with `item_done()`. If the driver uses `get()` instead of `get_next_item()`, `get()` calls `item_done()` itself, so `finish_item()` returns as soon as the driver takes the item, before it is driven.

Randomizing between the two calls lets the item depend on the state of the bench at the moment it is granted. Sequences nest: a parent's `body()` can start child sequences on the same or other sequencers, and every item still goes through the sequencer's arbitration one at a time.

