A component can have only one `write()` method, so two analysis imps of the same type need different method names. The `uvm_analysis_imp_decl` macro defines a new imp class that calls `write<suffix>()`:

```systemverilog
`uvm_analysis_imp_decl(_exp)   // package scope: defines class uvm_analysis_imp_exp
`uvm_analysis_imp_decl(_act)   // defines class uvm_analysis_imp_act

class bus_scoreboard extends uvm_scoreboard;
  `uvm_component_utils(bus_scoreboard)
  uvm_analysis_imp_exp #(bus_item, bus_scoreboard) exp_imp;
  uvm_analysis_imp_act #(bus_item, bus_scoreboard) act_imp;
  bus_item expected[$];

  function new(string name, uvm_component parent);
    super.new(name, parent);
    exp_imp = new("exp_imp", this);
    act_imp = new("act_imp", this);
  endfunction

  function void write_exp(bus_item t);
    expected.push_back(t);
  endfunction

  function void write_act(bus_item t);
    bus_item e;
    if (expected.size() == 0) begin
      `uvm_error("SCB/UNEXP", {"no expected item for ", t.convert2string()})
      return;
    end
    e = expected.pop_front();
    if (!t.compare(e))
      `uvm_error("SCB/MISMATCH", {"exp ", e.convert2string(), " act ", t.convert2string()})
  endfunction
endclass

// env connect_phase: initiator port -> target imp
predictor.ap.connect(scb.exp_imp);
out_agt.mon.ap.connect(scb.act_imp);
```

The three connection objects:

1. A port is on the initiator. It requires an interface and calls methods through it, such as `put`, `get` or `write`.

2. An imp is on the target. It ends the chain and calls the methods its owner implements.

3. An export passes an interface from an inner component out through its parent, so a parent can offer its child's imp as its own.

`connect()` is always called on the side closer to the initiator: port to export or imp, child port to parent port when going up, parent export to child export or imp when going down.

Imp versus FIFO: with an imp, `write_act()` runs inside the monitor's thread at the moment of the write, so it must not block. Connecting the monitor to a `uvm_tlm_analysis_fifo` instead (its `analysis_export`) buffers items. The scoreboard then pulls them in its own `run_phase` with a blocking `get()`, which suits comparisons that must wait, for example for a late expected item. The analysis FIFO is unbounded. A plain `uvm_tlm_fifo` defaults to size 1, so a `put()` into a full one blocks. A FIFO buffers; it adds no parallelism by itself.

