```systemverilog
class bus_item extends uvm_sequence_item;
  `uvm_object_utils(bus_item)
  rand bit [31:0] addr;
  rand bit [31:0] data;
  rand bit        write;
  int unsigned    latency;     // bookkeeping: excluded from equality on purpose

  function new(string name = "bus_item");
    super.new(name);
  endfunction

  function void do_copy(uvm_object rhs);
    bus_item r;
    if (!$cast(r, rhs))
      `uvm_fatal("BADCAST", "do_copy: rhs is not a bus_item")
    super.do_copy(rhs);
    addr = r.addr;  data = r.data;  write = r.write;  latency = r.latency;
  endfunction

  function bit do_compare(uvm_object rhs, uvm_comparer comparer);
    bus_item r;
    if (!$cast(r, rhs)) return 0;
    return super.do_compare(rhs, comparer) &&
           addr == r.addr && write == r.write && data == r.data;
  endfunction

  function string convert2string();
    return $sformatf("%s addr=0x%08h data=0x%08h lat=%0d",
                     write ? "WR" : "RD", addr, data, latency);
  endfunction
endclass
```

Handle assignment, `b2 = b1`, copies the handle: both names refer to one object. `b2.copy(b1)` copies the contents into an existing `b2`, through field automation and then your `do_copy()`. `clone()` creates a new object of the same type and copies into it. It returns a `uvm_object`, so you need `$cast(b2, b1.clone())`. How deep a copy goes for object-type fields is up to your `do_copy()` or the field-macro flags; `copy()` is not deep by default for everything.

`compare()` only returns a bit. A miscompare does not raise `UVM_ERROR` by itself (uvm-core's default comparer reports miscompares at UVM_INFO), so the caller must check the result and report the error.

Field macros (`uvm_object_utils_begin`, `uvm_field_int(addr, UVM_ALL_ON)`, `uvm_field_int(latency, UVM_ALL_ON | UVM_NOCOMPARE)`, `uvm_object_utils_end`) generate print, copy, compare, pack and record from one list. They are less code, but the generic machinery runs on every call, which costs time in hot paths (the size of that cost depends on the implementation), and the flags are easy to get wrong. Hand-written `do_*` methods are explicit, fast and easy to debug, at the cost of more code. The two can coexist: `copy()` runs the field automation first, then calls `do_copy()`.

