```systemverilog
// interrupt monitor (component)
uvm_event irq_ev;
function void build_phase(uvm_phase phase);
  irq_ev = uvm_event_pool::get_global("dma_irq");
endfunction
task run_phase(uvm_phase phase);
  forever begin
    @(posedge vif.irq);
    irq_ev.trigger();
  end
endtask

// virtual sequence
uvm_event irq_ev = uvm_event_pool::get_global("dma_irq");
start_dma_transfer();
irq_ev.wait_ptrigger();     // still returns if the trigger happened earlier in this time step
```

A `uvm_event` is a class wrapper around a SystemVerilog event. It adds optional data passed with `trigger(data)`, an on/off state (`wait_on`, `wait_off`, `reset`), a trigger count and time, and callbacks. Because it is an object, any component or sequence can share it. `uvm_event_pool::get_global("name")` returns the same event for the same name anywhere in the bench, so the monitor and the sequence need no hierarchical path to each other.

The race: `wait_trigger()` is `@event` underneath. If the waiter starts waiting after the trigger in the same time step, it misses that trigger and may wait forever. `wait_ptrigger()` first checks the event's `triggered` state, which persists until the end of the time step, and returns at once. The plain SV equivalents are `@ev` and `wait(ev.triggered)`.

A `uvm_barrier` is a rendezvous: each process calls `wait_for()`, and all are released together once the threshold count of waiters arrives. Use it for "all agents finished reset", not for one-to-one notification.

The side effect: `uvm_pool::get(key)` creates an entry if the key does not exist. For the object pools, which include `uvm_event_pool`, it constructs a new object under that name. A misspelled name therefore creates a second event that nobody triggers, and the waiter hangs. Define event names as constants, and use `exists()` when you only want to check.

