```systemverilog
class top_vsqr extends uvm_sequencer;       // a container of handles; arbitrates no items
  `uvm_component_utils(top_vsqr)
  apb_sequencer apb_sqr;
  axi_sequencer axi_sqr;
  function new(string name, uvm_component parent);
    super.new(name, parent);
  endfunction
endclass

// env connect_phase: point the handles at the agents' sequencers
vsqr.apb_sqr = apb_agt.sqr;
vsqr.axi_sqr = axi_agt.sqr;

class cfg_then_traffic_vseq extends uvm_sequence;
  `uvm_object_utils(cfg_then_traffic_vseq)
  `uvm_declare_p_sequencer(top_vsqr)       // declares p_sequencer and $casts m_sequencer into it

  function new(string name = "cfg_then_traffic_vseq");
    super.new(name);
  endfunction

  task body();
    apb_cfg_seq   cfg = apb_cfg_seq::type_id::create("cfg");
    axi_burst_seq rd  = axi_burst_seq::type_id::create("rd");
    axi_burst_seq wr  = axi_burst_seq::type_id::create("wr");
    cfg.start(p_sequencer.apb_sqr, this);   // configure first; 'this' makes it a child
    fork                                    // then two AXI streams in parallel
      rd.start(p_sequencer.axi_sqr, this);
      wr.start(p_sequencer.axi_sqr, this);
    join                                    // body ends only when both finish
  endtask
endclass

// test run_phase, inside the objection:
vseq.start(env.vsqr);
```

What the pieces do:

1. The virtual sequencer is just a component that holds handles to the real sequencers. No item ever goes through it.

2. The order of operations lives in the virtual sequence's `body()`, not in the sequencer.

3. `uvm_declare_p_sequencer(top_vsqr)` declares `p_sequencer` and `$cast`s `m_sequencer` into it when the sequence starts. Starting the sequence on the wrong sequencer type fatals with DCLPSQ.

4. Passing `this` as the parent makes the child inherit the parent's priority and appear under it in the sequence hierarchy.

5. `join`, not `join_none`, keeps `body()` (and so the test's objection) alive until both streams finish.

The alternative: the virtual sequence holds the sequencer handles itself (`apb_sequencer apb_sqr; axi_sequencer axi_sqr;`), the test assigns them from the env, and the test starts it with `vseq.start(null)`. There is no extra component and no `p_sequencer` cast, but the test must know the env's structure, and there is no sequencer on which to set a default sequence or take a lock. Choose it for small benches. Choose a virtual sequencer when several tests and sub-environments share the handle wiring, or when you nest virtual sequencers in a layered SoC environment.

With the default policy, `UVM_SEQ_ARB_FIFO`, priority is ignored: requests are granted in arrival order. Arbitration is per item, not per sequence, so items from the three sequences interleave.

Priority counts only in the priority-aware modes, set with `set_arbitration()`:

1. `UVM_SEQ_ARB_WEIGHTED`: random choice, weighted by priority.

2. `UVM_SEQ_ARB_STRICT_FIFO`: highest priority first, FIFO among equal priorities.

3. `UVM_SEQ_ARB_STRICT_RANDOM`: highest priority first, random among equals.

4. `UVM_SEQ_ARB_USER`: your `user_priority_arbitration()` decides.

A root sequence's default priority is 100, and a child inherits its parent's. Set it with `start(sqr, parent, priority)`, per item with `start_item(item, priority)`, or with `set_priority()`. Strict modes can starve low-priority traffic for as long as high-priority requests keep arriving.

```systemverilog
env.agt.sqr.set_arbitration(UVM_SEQ_ARB_STRICT_FIFO);
fork
  bg_seq.start(env.agt.sqr, null, 100);
  burst_seq.start(env.agt.sqr, null, 500);
join_none

// interrupt-service sequence
task body();
  grab();                      // to the front of the queue; blocks other sequences once granted
  repeat (3) begin
    isr_item it = isr_item::type_id::create("it");
    start_item(it);
    if (!it.randomize()) `uvm_error("RNDFAIL", "isr_item randomize failed")
    finish_item(it);
  end
  ungrab();
endtask
```

`lock()` and `grab()` both give one sequence exclusive use of the sequencer, so its items go out back to back. The difference is when they take effect. A lock request is arbitrated like any other request and is granted after the earlier requests complete. A grab request goes to the front of the arbitration queue and is granted as soon as no other lock or grab blocks it. Use `grab()` for urgent work such as interrupt service, and `lock()` for an atomic sequence that may wait its turn. While a lock is held, only the holder and its child sequences are granted.

Pitfalls: a holder that waits for something only another sequence on the same sequencer can produce will deadlock. Always release with `unlock()` or `ungrab()`; uvm-core also removes a sequence's locks when it exits, but relying on that hides intent. Use `is_relevant()` with `wait_for_relevant()` to make a sequence temporarily ineligible without locking anyone out.

